        </figure>

        <div class="h2-box">
</section>

<!-- HOW AEROSOL ACTUATORS WORK -->
<section class="section" id="how-aerosol-actuators-work">
  <h2>How Aerosol Actuators Work</h2>
  </div>

        <p>
            Aerosol actuators operate by transmitting mechanical force from the user’s finger to the valve stem, opening
            the valve and allowing pressurized contents to flow upward. Once inside the actuator, the formulation passes
            through internal channels, expansion regions, and orifices that control velocity and atomization.
        </p>

        <p>
            The interaction between actuator geometry, propellant pressure, formulation viscosity, and valve flow rate
            determines whether the discharge appears as a fine mist, coarse spray, foam, or solid stream.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Stem socket that engages the valve stem</li>
            <li>Internal flow channels and swirl chambers</li>
            <li>Discharge orifice controlling exit velocity</li>
        </ul>

        <figure style="margin:24px 0;text-align:center;">
            
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/aerosol-actuator-cutaway-internal-geometry-IoYLJhsdno443OH2.png" 
          alt="Cutaway view of an aerosol actuator showing internal geometry" 
          width="1200" 
          height="800" 
          loading="lazy" 
        />
      </div>
      
            <figcaption style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#555;margin-top:8px;">Figure 3: Cutaway view of an aerosol
                actuator illustrating internal flow paths, swirl chamber, and discharge orifice.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="h2-box">
</section>

<!-- TYPES OF AEROSOL ACTUATORS -->
<section class="section" id="types-of-aerosol-actuators">
  <h2>Types of Aerosol Actuators</h2>
  </div>

        <p>
            Aerosol actuators are classified according to the type of discharge they produce and the application they
            are designed to serve. Each actuator type incorporates specific internal geometries to match formulation
            properties and end-use requirements. Selection must always consider the entire aerosol system rather than
            the actuator in isolation.
        </p>

        <p>
            Different industries rely on different actuator categories to achieve required spray characteristics, safety
            features, and regulatory compliance.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Standard spray actuators</li>
            <li>Foam and mousse actuators</li>
            <li>Stream or jet actuators</li>
            <li>Metered-dose actuators</li>
            <li>Specialty actuators such as inverted or locking designs</li>
        </ul>

        <figure style="margin:24px 0;text-align:center;">
            
